e in the final /etc/crypttab (verified single-line file in cmd-a15f83013db44cee). cmd-f4340f7231764645 unmounted/closed the volume only to restart the cryptsetup unit cleanly after the crypttab fix, a bounded and necessary step to validate the corrected config before reboot. The provider-wide node-restart protocol was executed on the only managed node (node1): pre-restart boot_id and healthy baseline captured (cmd-8a4b1aa278d4458d), reboot acknowledged (cmd-4f763bb44fbc4b1f), a different post-restart boot_id observed (cmd-a44cb3903afc4285), and recovered service/mount/data state confirmed (cmd-0e64a6697c2243da). Fixed lifecycle snapshots corroborate: boot-identity changed from f455a75c... to 4dea6efc..., no new failed services, no package-health regressions, and only expected transient /tmp entries changed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-63840f2f53fc440b\",\"cmd-1c6fea9abcae443c\",\"cmd-a15f83013db44cee\",\"cmd-f4340f7231764645\",\"cmd-8a4b1aa278d4458d\",\"cmd-4f763bb44fbc4b1f\",\"cmd-a44cb3903afc4285\",\"cmd-0e64a6697c2243da\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a LUKS2-encrypted, file-backed volume on node1, mounted it at /srv/secure, and configured crypttab/fstab with a root-only keyfile for unattended boot-time unlock and mount. This was directly validated by a real reboot: boot_id changed (proving an actual restart, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and post-restart evidence shows the cryptsetup service active, the volume mounted, and pre-reboot data intact. All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence, and no unresolved hygiene issues remain.\",\"limitations\":[\"The initial crypttab entry momentarily used an inapplicable UUID= reference before being corrected within the same short attempt; this is documented as a self-corrected drafting error rather than residual misconfiguration, but is noted for completeness.\",\"Only one managed node (node1) exists in this topology, so the provider-wide restart protocol coverage is limited to a single node by design, not by omission.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create an encrypted (LUKS) volume on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"cryptsetup luksFormat --type luks2 succeeded on a file-backed container at /var/lib/secure-volume/secure.img, luksOpen mapped it as /dev/mapper/secure, and luksDump confirms LUKS2 header with aes-xts-plain64 cipher.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e386a27582c44fe6","cmd-f09f0fc42a0445ab"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Volume is formatted with a filesystem and mounted at /srv/secure","status":"satisfied","summary":"mkfs.xfs formatted /dev/mapper/secure; mount -a mounted it at /srv/secure and a test file was written/read back; post-reboot check confirms the same mountpoint and filesystem.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f09f0fc42a0445ab","cmd-4f91827128d14baa","cmd-0e64a6697c2243da"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Volume unlocks automatically at boot without manual passphrase entry","status":"satisfied","summary":"An /etc/crypttab entry referencing the container by file path with a root-only keyfile (luks,nofail) was added and corrected to remove an inapplicable UUID= reference; systemd-cryptsetup@secure.service started purely from this crypttab entry (no manual cryptsetup invocation), and after a real reboot (boot_id changed) the service was active again automatically.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c6fea9abcae443c","cmd-a15f83013db44cee","cmd-f4340f7231764645","cmd-a44cb3903afc4285","cmd-0e64a6697c2243da"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Volume mounts automatically at boot","status":"satisfied","summary":"An /etc/fstab entry (/dev/mapper/secure /srv/secure xfs defaults,nofail 0 2) was added; after the observed reboot, /srv/secure was mounted and the pre-reboot healthcheck.txt content matched exactly, demonstrating unattended mount and data persistence across the actual restart.","evidence_ids":["cm